🚀 Feature
Rather than try to invent the "best" API for defining gradients in C++, it is probably easier for people to use an API that looks almost exactly like the Python API, with the same restrictions and behavior. Example:
// Same API as the python interface, provided by us
class AutogradContext {
	void save_for_backward(at::ArrayRef<at::Tensor> tensors);
    void mark_dirty(at::ArrayRef<at::Tensor> tensors);
    void mark_non_differentiable(at::ArrayRef<at::Tensor> tensors);
private:
   std::SmallVector<SavedVariable, 4> saved_for_backward; 
};
template<typename Policy>
class CustomFunction {
   // some template processing so that we generate an apply function with the same types
   // as Policy::forward but without the ctx argument.
   template<typename... Arg>
   static return_type_of<Policy::forward> apply(Arg... args);
};

// User writes the following,
// looks basically the same as the PyTorch equivalent.
class MyReLU : public torch::autograd::CustomFunction<MyReLU> {
	// args/returns can be whatever the user wants
	static at::Tensor forward(AutogradContext& ctx, at::Tensor input) {
		ctx.save_for_backward({input});
		return input.clamp(0);
	}
	static at::Tensor(AutogradContext& ctx, at::Tensor grad_output) {
		at::Tensor input = ctx.saved_tensors(0);
		at::Tensor grad_input = grad_output.clone();
		grad_input[input < 0] = 0
		return grad_input;
	}
};

// you can register MyReLU::apply as a normal custom operator, or call it directly from C++

Motivation
We currently do not have a user-exposed way to write gradients from C++. Our internal API has too many corner-cases to expose directly.
